## What is wrong

The encoder is built with `utf8 => 1` (`lib/MCP/ArcRaiders.pm:28`), so `_to_json` (`:47-50`) returns UTF-8 bytes. Every tool returns that string as its result (`:156`, `:199`, `:224`, `:249`, `:265`, `:299`), MCP::Server wraps it as a text block and its transports encode the response with Mojo::JSON, which expects characters. Non-ASCII text is therefore encoded twice.

## Reproduce

    my $a = MCP::ArcRaiders->new;
    my $t = $a->json->encode({ name => "Raider\x{2019}s Caf\x{e9}" });
    my $back = Mojo::JSON::decode_json(Mojo::JSON::encode_json({ text => $t }))->{text};
    # client sees: { "name" : "Raiderâs CafÃ©" }

Item and quest descriptions carry typographic apostrophes, dashes and accented names; all of them arrive garbled. `t/api.t:14` builds its own decoder with `utf8 => 0` and the fixtures are compared as Perl data, so the suite never sees the wire form. MCP::Picnic has the identical defect (reported there); fix both the same way.

## Done

- Encoder for tool results uses `utf8 => 0` (characters out); `bin/mcp-arcraiders-http` keeps rendering through Mojolicious' `json =>`.
- A test sends a `tools/call` through the server's `handle` and the transport encoding with a fixture containing `’` and `é`, and asserts the client-side text.

